WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.020
How can you watch chemistry happen inside a living body without opening it?

2
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:11.800
Positron emission tomography, or PET, tracks radioactive tracers to map activity

3
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.100
inside living tissue.

4
00:00:15.767 --> 00:00:18.847
Zoom into one cell to see the classic scan's trick.

5
00:00:20.187 --> 00:00:23.827
In glycolysis, the breakdown of glucose, step 1 is a trap.

6
00:00:25.047 --> 00:00:29.407
The enzyme hexokinase moves a phosphate from ATP onto glucose.

7
00:00:30.707 --> 00:00:34.407
That phosphate's negative charge traps glucose inside the cell.

8
00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:42.090
FDG-PET uses a glucose look-alike, or analog, labeled with radioactive fluorine-18.

9
00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:47.250
It is trapped after step 1, and the scanner detects its radiation.

10
00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:51.190
So tissues taking up glucose fast light up.

11
00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:57.270
Many tumors take up glucose fast even with oxygen present, the Warburg effect.

12
00:00:58.383 --> 00:01:01.603
Picture a bright spot on a patient's FDG-PET scan.

13
00:01:02.843 --> 00:01:04.823
Those cells are pulling in glucose fast.

14
00:01:06.243 --> 00:01:09.083
Predict: does that bright spot prove the tissue is a tumor?

15
00:01:14.717 --> 00:01:19.077
No: FDG-PET reports glucose uptake, not cancer itself.

16
00:01:20.457 --> 00:01:22.997
Inflamed tissue also takes up glucose fast.

17
00:01:24.397 --> 00:01:28.477
In a cold adult, heat-making brown fat can light up on PET-CT too.

18
00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:34.407
Other PET tracers track enzymes or protein deposits in living brains.

19
00:01:35.607 --> 00:01:41.107
In patients with probable Alzheimer disease, PET showed the drug donepezil cutting

20
00:01:41.107 --> 00:01:43.507
cortical acetylcholinesterase activity.

21
00:01:44.587 --> 00:01:46.887
That enzyme breaks down acetylcholine.

22
00:01:47.927 --> 00:01:50.947
That measures mechanism, not clinical benefit.

23
00:01:52.127 --> 00:01:56.867
The guiding idea: PET maps living chemistry, not whether a person benefits.

24
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.240
The glucose-analog idea unlocked three findings.

25
00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:08.200
In 1988, Tootell and colleagues mapped macaque visual cortex, V1, with

26
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.040
2-deoxyglucose, which marks active cells.

27
00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:17.500
Near the fovea, the centre of gaze, about 15 millimetres of cortex served each

28
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:23.520
degree. Estimates vary by animal and method, so 15 is a macaque value.

29
00:02:24.583 --> 00:02:30.403
In 1994, Luc Pellerin and Pierre Magistretti gave glutamate to cultured mouse

30
00:02:30.403 --> 00:02:32.403
astrocytes, glial cells.

31
00:02:33.643 --> 00:02:37.603
The cells took up more 2-deoxyglucose and released more lactate.

32
00:02:38.923 --> 00:02:41.523
Blocking the sodium pump with ouabain blocked this.

33
00:02:42.703 --> 00:02:48.023
Their explanation: glutamate arrives with sodium, and pumping it out costs ATP,

34
00:02:48.403 --> 00:02:49.883
which drives glycolysis.

35
00:02:49.883 --> 00:02:53.923
They proposed that the lactate fuels active neurons.

36
00:02:55.117 --> 00:03:02.097
Finally, the 2023 A4 study tracked amyloid, a plaque protein, for 240 weeks.

37
00:03:03.377 --> 00:03:07.797
Adults with elevated amyloid got an antibody, solanezumab, or placebo.

38
00:03:09.317 --> 00:03:15.617
Amyloid PET uses centiloids: 0 is the average plaque-free young adult, 100 is

39
00:03:15.617 --> 00:03:16.997
typical Alzheimer dementia.

40
00:03:18.117 --> 00:03:23.477
Amyloid rose 11.6 centiloids with drug, 19.3 with placebo.

41
00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:30.180
Subtract 11.6 from 19.3, then divide by 19.3.

42
00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:35.640
That equals about 0.40, or 40% % slower buildup.

43
00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:41.280
Yet a slower-rising imaging measure is not slower decline in the person.

44
00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:45.280
PET maps chemistry; benefit needs other proof.
